  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHit or miss showers and thunderstorms through the weekend. \nSome may be severe, with the best chance of this on Sunday. Any \nstronger storm could produce locally heavy rainfall, which could\ncause localized flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA series of weak low-mid level shortwaves pass over the top of \nan northeast extension of the Bermuda High, and gradually weaken\nit through tonight. This allows for a stronger northern stream \nshortwave trough to approach later tonight, then cross the area \non Sunday. At the surface, a series of weak troughs of low \npressure cross the region through tonight. A cold front \napproaches Sunday morning then moves through western Maine \nSunday afternoon.\n\nThere will be two main focusing mechanisms for convection today,\nthe passing troughs of low pressure and the sea breeze front.\nWhen those two intersect, you will see the highest likelihood\nfor precipitation and most likely the strongest localized\nstorms. This places the best threat for more widespread showers\nand storms over Downeast Maine and the Bangor Region.\n\nIn general you should see around 750-1500 J/kg of CAPE along\nwith with 10-20kt of bulk shear. The low values of shear should\nkeep the vast majority, if not all storms today below severe\nlimits. With storm motion of 5-15Kt and precipitable waters \nmainly around 1.5-1.75 inches - there is the risk for locally \nheavy rainfall over a concentrated area. This could result in \nlocalized flooding and is the main threat for today.\n\nOn Sunday, storm motion increases to around 20 kt, so flooding\nshould be a more limited threat. However, CAPE increases to\n1000-2000 J/kg and shear increases to 20-30kt. This increases\nthe risk of strong to possibly severe thunderstorms.\nAccordingly, SPC has most of Northern and Eastern Maine in a\nMarginal (level 1 of 5) risk for severe thunderstorms, with the\nmain threat localized damaging wind gusts. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered rain showers and isolated thunderstorms early\nnext week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nRounds of shortwaves move through the north early next week, \nbringing a chance for rain showers and some thunderstorms. \nThunder possible, mainly in areas north of Bangor, Monday night \ninto Tuesday. Environment does not look too impressive for \nstrong thunderstorms with limited instability, so could just be \nsome brief isolated storms below severe criteria.",
